Guest input over a pipe: a builtin call with input wrote it to a temp file under
TMPDIR for the child's stdin, so the single-threaded parent never had to feed
input and drain output at once; every such call created and deleted a file, a
guest that died early leaked one, and the file column of every benchmark carried
the churn. The parent now polls the input pipe's write end beside the output
pipe's read end and interleaves the two, closing the write end when the input is
spent, with SIGPIPE ignored for the feed so a guest that stops reading early ends
the feed rather than make.

